it's pretty clear by now that apple is  planning on bringing 5g to their iphone  12 lineup which they should reveal about  two months from now  and some rumors are even pointing to  apple giving you the ability to choose  between 4g  and a more expensive 5g version of the  two  non-pro iphone 12 models now no matter  if you'll truly get the option or not  there's still this question that you  need to answer for yourself  do i really need 5g so what i'm gonna do  in this video is discuss the real world  benefits that i noticed using a 5g phone  compared to my 4g iphone and i'm also  going to talk about what other people  think of 5g  and ultimately how much it should affect  your buying decision  first of all you need to understand that  not all 5g is the same  their sub 6 5g and millimeter wave 5g  but before i discuss the differences i  want to talk about the success  of two popular smartphone brands that  have recently switched to 5g  let's start with samsung and the galaxy  s20 line  ever since they launched the s20 lineup  it's been rumored that sales of  samsung's s20 phones  were way down compared to last year's  galaxy s10  which shipments dropping by 32.6 percent  now of course we should definitely blame  some of that on the pandemic for the low  sales  since the entire smartphone industry  went down but  one thing is for certain the addition of  5g  made the s20 line very expensive at a  thousand dollars for the  entry level model so android authority  ran a poll  asking people why they didn't upgrade to  the s20  and about 71 of the people said that it  was just  too expensive and then the other 11  said that they don't need 5g and that  basically means that the addition of 5g  just wasn't worth the extra price for  all of those people  and to further prove the point it's been  rumored that samsung is  exercising caution on the note 20 parts  to try to keep the price down  there was even a mention that this was  specifically for the galaxy  note 20 5g model since 5g makes it  much more expensive now the oneplus 8  pro had a ton of hype thanks to the  exciting leaks  and the success of the previous oneplus  7 pro but there were a lot of people  that were disappointed by the high price  about a week before launch 9 to 5 google  made a poll asking users  how much would they be willing to spend  on the oneplus 8 pro  and almost half of them said 700  the price turned out to be from 900 to a  thousand dollars which only about  10 said that they are willing to pay  according to the poll  and of course the high price was mostly  attributed to the addition of 5g  so the point that i'm trying to make is  that 5g is causing phones to get much  more  expensive than people expect and there  are now rumors pointing to the iphone 12  being priced at least 50  more expensive than the iphone 11 prices  despite  not having ear pods or a charger in the  box  so now the question is if apple does  give you the choice of getting a 5g  model  should you pay the extra price i've been  using apple's iphone 11 pro max for  almost a full year now  which obviously uses 4g so i'm gonna  give you guys my experience  of this iphone compared to the galaxy  s20 ultra  which is the most expensive mainstream  5g  phone i currently use t-mobile here in  spokane washington  and 4g has mostly worked fine for me for  everything from web browsing to using  apps  i've really only noticed a couple of  issues with connectivity  the main issue i've noticed so far is  occasional hiccups when i'm trying to  stream youtube videos  there's also the fact that i start to  lose service going further away from the  city  and sometimes in certain dead zones of  the city other than that the only other  issue  is that reception isn't that great when  i'm inside of larger buildings like my  church  or target for example now let's talk  about my experience with 5g  a couple months ago i switched to the  galaxy s20 ultra so that i could give it  a proper review  and since i have t-mobile i don't have  access to the ultra fast  millimeter wave 5g in my city just the  sub 6 5g  that's because right now there are only  a small amount of cities that actually  have millimeter 5g towers  and to get the great speeds you actually  have to be  very close to those towers that's  because it works using  a very specific part of the radio  frequency spectrum  between 24 gigahertz and 100 gigahertz  which have a very short wavelength this  basically allows more bandwidth or data  to be transferred but the issue is that  it doesn't travel very far  and it doesn't penetrate walls well at  all and this is also a type of 5g which  is the most controversial  with many people concerned about the  health impacts of the shortwave radio  frequency  now sub 65g on the other hand has a lot  more in common with 4g  than it does with millimeter wave 5g for  example  t-mobile was able to upgrade their cell  towers to sub 6 relatively quickly and  easily  all across the us since it uses a much  longer  wavelength millimeter wave 5g requires a  lot more work  and a fiber optic internet connection  which is why it's taking so long to  implement it  so even though the galaxy s20 ultra  supports both sub 6 and millimeter wave  i only have access to t-mobile sub 6  here in spokane so here was  my experience with it it was fantastic  there were a few spots where i typically  had just one bar  and data was pretty much unusable and in  those cases  the s20 ultra with sub 65g would give me  two bars at times maybe even three and  it would work just fine  browsing the web was super snappy and i  could consistently play back youtube  videos at 1440p  with no buffering or hiccups keep in  mind my iphone had issues at times  even with 720p and i rarely tried  1080. on top of that in areas with a  good signal my data speeds were about  double the speed testing him at the same  location at the same time just pulling  the sim from one  and putting it into the other and during  congestion  up to six times faster as you guys could  see here  let me just say going back to my iphone  4g  was noticeable after the s20 ultra  most people probably won't get much use  out of that millimeter wave 5g  for at least a couple years so if you're  thinking about buying the pro model  iphone 12  just for that reason it probably doesn't  make sense  now sub six 5g is definitely worth it  since it gives you better reception  overall  faster speeds and it allowed me to  stream youtube without a hiccup  even at 1440p which was awesome now  in my opinion i still think apple needs  to add 5g to all of the phones this year  i don't think it makes sense to have  multiple versions of the phones at  different price points  now they could wait another year but  then they will be  really a ways behind the competition so  as long as apple can keep the price of  their phones the same  they should switch to 5g and be done  with it  and luckily most rumors are pointing to  the price staying very close  and that's probably because apple will  likely no longer be shipping earpods and  even a charger in the box  which in my opinion is a worthwhile  trade-off  realistically even if we don't need 5g  this year  most iphone users keep their phones for  3 years or more now  so it would be really nice to have it be  future proof  if so we could end up with another  iphone 6s  situation where it is so good that it  becomes the standard  long term for a lot of people if this  video is helpful  go ahead and tap that like button and  click the circle above to subscribe  and also be sure to check out our iphone  12 leaks and rumors video  right over there thank you guys for  watching and i'll see you  in the next video  [Music]  you
 

